000-000000000001","dimension":"failureConsequence","proposedScore":48,"anchorTier":40,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Forward Fall Single-Step Split-Stance Catch Hold task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"98000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"impact","proposedScore":24,"anchorTier":20,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Forward Fall Single-Step Split-Stance Catch Hold task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."},{"variantId":"98000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001","dimension":"workCapacityDemand","proposedScore":15,"anchorTier":20,"rationale":"Candidate anchor generated from the exact Forward Fall Single-Step Split-Stance Catch Hold task contract. Independent human calibration remains required."}],"evidence":[{"sectionKey":"identity","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22233797/","sourceTitle":"The difference is in the start: impact of timing and start procedure on sprint running performance","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["Start procedure and start position materially affect measured sprint performance, while metres, yards, and a finish marker describe dosage and logistics rather than a new movement identity.","Falling Start Position Hold must state whether the athlete falls and accelerates through a distance or catches the fall and holds without sprinting; generic speed, neural, approach, and cone wording does not create a separate exercise."]},{"sectionKey":"taxonomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8998119/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical Performance Factors in the Track and Field Sprint Start: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["The start, push-off, and first acceleration steps are related but distinguishable phases of short sprint acceleration.","Falling Start Position Hold is either a standing falling-start short acceleration or a no-sprint fall-to-split-stance control task; measurement unit, finish cone, and a 5-to-10-yard range remain delivery data."]},{"sectionKey":"anatomy","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4689850/","sourceTitle":"Sprint Acceleration Mechanics: The Major Role of Hamstrings in Horizontal Force Production","sourcePublisher":"Frontiers in Physiology","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Sprint acceleration uses coordinated hip, knee, and ankle actions, with hamstring contribution to horizontally oriented force production.","The acceleration card should identify gluteal, hamstring, quadriceps, plantar-flexor, hip-flexor, foot, lumbopelvic, and arm-action roles; the catch-and-hold version shifts emphasis toward braking, single-leg stance control, and trunk stabilization."]},{"sectionKey":"biomechanics","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8998119/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical Performance Factors in the Track and Field Sprint Start: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["Early acceleration performance is associated with forward-directed impulse, propulsive force, and progressive projection through the first steps.","The forward fall is an initiation constraint, not proof of an ideal universal body angle; require a controlled whole-body lean, a timely recovery step, continued acceleration through the target, and an anthropometrically appropriate setup."]},{"sectionKey":"difficulty","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22233797/","sourceTitle":"The difference is in the start: impact of timing and start procedure on sprint running performance","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["Different start procedures create different timing and body-position effects and should not share one generic difficulty value.","Exercise difficulty must directly score technical complexity, physical and absolute-load demand, coordination, supervision, failure consequence, impact, work-capacity demand, and overall difficulty; athlete experience remains programming context."]},{"sectionKey":"load_fatigue_recovery","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6407765/","sourceTitle":"Correlations between muscle-tendon parameters and acceleration ability in 20 m sprints","sourcePublisher":"PLOS ONE","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":84,"claims":["Power output is highest during the first steps of sprint acceleration, so a short distance does not make maximal starts negligible load.","Falling Start Position Hold needs distance, intent, timed or untimed status, total high-speed contacts, surface, footwear, posterior-chain and plantar-flexor stress, technical-fatigue markers, recovery, and session sprint-volume accounting."]},{"sectionKey":"constraints","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/education/nsca-coach/nsca-coach-5.4.pdf","sourceTitle":"Developing Linear Speed","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Quality sprint work requires a suitable surface, marked start and finish, sufficient run-out, and recovery that preserves speed quality.","Require a clear level dry lane, safe separation, suitable footwear, a finish and run-out zone, and readiness to control the forward fall; a cone may mark distance but does not define the exercise."]},{"sectionKey":"dosage","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/education/nsca-coach/nsca-coach-5.4.pdf","sourceTitle":"Developing Linear Speed","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Acceleration sessions should prescribe distance, intensity, repetitions, sets, and recovery rather than repetitions alone.","For high-intent falling starts, use 2-6 efforts over 5-20 metres with approximately 60-180 seconds recovery according to distance and athlete response; prescribe the catch-and-hold task by clean attempts and hold seconds, not sprint distance."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8998119/","sourceTitle":"Biomechanical Performance Factors in the Track and Field Sprint Start: A Systematic Review","sourcePublisher":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["The setup should support rapid hip extension and forward propulsion without forcing an inappropriate first-step geometry.","Use a small cue set: stand as one line, let the lean develop without bending at the waist, recover before a stumble, push the ground backward, coordinate the arms, accelerate through the target, and use the full run-out."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Training should use qualified supervision, correct technique, manageable demands, and gradual progression.","Stop for pain, limping, repeated stumble, an early reaching step, waist break, head-first fall, unsafe surface or spacing, loss of projection or arm-leg coordination, marked speed decline, collision risk, or failure to decelerate in the run-out."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/education/nsca-coach/nsca-coach-5.4.pdf","sourceTitle":"Developing Linear Speed","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Acceleration and maximal-velocity sprinting are distinct qualities and quality sprint work should not be converted into dense conditioning.","Falling Start Position Hold belongs after preparation and before fatiguing work; use the catch-and-hold task as a lower-speed initiation and position constraint, not as an equivalent dose of maximal acceleration."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://worldathletics.org/download/downloadnsa?filename=a0cae133-1056-4b89-9f93-16d87fd3bbd4.pdf&urlslug=introduction-to-sprinting","sourceTitle":"Introduction to Sprinting","sourcePublisher":"World Athletics","sourceKind":"governing_body","evidenceQuality":78,"claims":["Acceleration develops across successive steps rather than by immediately standing upright.","Show the whole-body fall, recovery threshold, first three contacts, target and run-out, an early-step regression, and the difference between accelerating through the target and catching the fall into a stable hold."]},{"sectionKey":"coach_support","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/education/nsca-coach/nsca-coach-5.4.pdf","sourceTitle":"Developing Linear Speed","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Coaches should distinguish acceleration quality from maximal velocity and conditioning.","Provide side and rear observation, start consistency, step and distance counts, timing method when used, projection and force-direction notes, arm-leg coordination, symptom and speed-loss stops, and exact merge or alias guidance."]},{"sectionKey":"accessibility","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/about-us/position-statements/youth-training-and-long-term-athletic-development/","sourceTitle":"Youth Training and Long-Term Athletic Development","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["Speed work should be developmentally appropriate and based on movement competency.","Accessibility options include a smaller lean, fall-to-step without sprint, two-point start instead of falling start, shorter distance, submaximal intent, visual start and finish marks, extra demonstration, untimed attempts, and longer recovery."]},{"sectionKey":"alternates","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22233797/","sourceTitle":"The difference is in the start: impact of timing and start procedure on sprint running performance","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":86,"claims":["Start position and timing procedure can change the task and measured result.","Five versus ten yards, ten yards versus ten metres, generic sprint wording, and a finish cone are dosage or logistics annotations; fall-to-hold, resisted starts, cue-response starts, and other starting positions require controlled variants or distinct definitions."]},{"sectionKey":"media","sourceUrl":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/171780?expand=PrivacyEnhancedMode&hl=en","sourceTitle":"Embed videos and playlists","sourcePublisher":"YouTube Help","sourceKind":"manufacturer_instruction","evidenceQuality":82,"claims":["YouTube supports privacy-enhanced embedding through youtube-nocookie.com.","The visible candidates for Falling Start Position Hold are candidate-only. Current availability and embedding, exact falling-start versus hold identity, complete viewing, captions, instructional quality, safety, reviewer identity, and approval remain separate gates; no candidate is approved by this packet."]}],"media":[{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MgXvvuMyFfc","videoId":"MgXvvuMyFfc","title":"Falling Start Speed Drill Improve Acceleration","channelName":"Developing Young Athletes Network","sourceQuery":"existing researched falling-start candidate","notes":"Sprint candidate; exact stop-and-hold sequence is unverified and quarantined.","linkStatus":"unverified","embeddingAllowed":false},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O_N0AHMoGFA","videoId":"O_N0AHMoGFA","title":"Speed Training Exercises: Falling Starts","channelName":"synergyfitnessteam","sourceQuery":"existing researched falling-start candidate","notes":"Adjacent falling-start component only; no hold approval.","linkStatus":"unverified","embeddingAllowed":false},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pYZ-4SoZNE8","videoId":"pYZ-4SoZNE8","title":"Speed Training | Falling Start","channelName":"ONEighty Athletics","sourceQuery":"existing researched falling-start candidate","notes":"Adjacent sprint candidate; terminal hold remains unverified.","linkStatus":"unverified","embeddingAllowed":false},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xxomffu3dTQ","videoId":"xxomffu3dTQ","title":"Falling Start Sprint","channelName":"Andrew Sacks","sourceQuery":"existing researched falling-start candidate","notes":"Non-exact sprint demonstration; quarantined for this card.","linkStatus":"unverified","embeddingAllowed":false},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xzJ96zkv3TI","videoId":"xzJ96zkv3TI","title":"Falling Starts","channelName":"Nick Brattain","sourceQuery":"existing researched falling-start candidate","notes":"Exact catch-and-hold sequence requires complete viewing and human review.","linkStatus":"unverified","embeddingAllowed":false}],"alternates":[{"name":"Falling Start 10m","classification":"new_definition","rationale":"The sprint continues through a target instead of ending in a hold.","distinguishingDimensions":{"terminalAction":"sprint"}},{"name":"Fall-to-Step Reset","classification":"modifier_annotation","rationale":"Removing the hold duration is a simpler dosage if the one-step catch remains.","distinguishingDimensions":{"holdSeconds":"zero"}},{"name":"Wall-Lean Split-Stance Hold","classification":"new_variant","rationale":"External support removes the free-fall and changes balance and force direction.","distinguishingDimensions":{"support":"wall"}},{"name":"Reactive Fall-to-Hold","classification":"new_variant","rationale":"An external signal controls release timing and adds response demand.","distinguishingDimensions":{"cue":"external"}},{"name":"Multi-Step Fall Catch","classification":"reject","rationale":"Multiple uncontrolled recovery steps indicate a failed attempt, not an alternate exercise.","distinguishingDimensions":{"qualityGate":"failed_single_step_catch"}}]}$card_1_payload$::JSONB);
